WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.330
Permission enforcement is a core requirement for private RAG.

00:00:03.330 --> 00:00:07.280
The system must not allow users to retrieve documents or chunks they could not access

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.360
through the approved source system or workflow.

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:12.735
This requirement applies even when the answer is summarized, transformed,

00:00:12.735 --> 00:00:15.400
or returned through a conversational interface.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.280
Enforcement can happen at ingestion time, query time, or both.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:22.230
Ingestion-time separation may reduce risk by placing different tenants, classifications,

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.965
or departments into different collections.

00:00:23.965 --> 00:00:28.480
Query-time enforcement is often essential because user access changes, documents move,

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:32.430
groups are updated, projects close, and workflow-specific restrictions may apply.

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:36.040
A strong design connects user identity, group membership, service accounts,

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:40.006
application roles, tenant IDs, document permissions, metadata filters,

00:00:40.006 --> 00:00:43.145
row-level or document-level rules, and audit trails.

00:00:43.145 --> 00:00:46.350
The retrieval service should know who is asking, what workflow they are using,

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:49.985
and what content is eligible before selecting context for the model.

00:00:49.985 --> 00:00:52.400
Administrative access deserves separate treatment.

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:55.020
People who manage indexes, prompts, connectors, logs,

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:59.335
or source mappings may have visibility and influence that ordinary users do not.

00:00:59.335 --> 00:01:01.795
Admin roles should be narrow, reviewable, and logged.

00:01:01.795 --> 00:01:05.985
Private RAG should favor least privilege and deny-by-default behavior for restricted

00:01:05.985 --> 00:01:06.705
material.

00:01:06.705 --> 00:01:10.570
Do not rely only on separate user interfaces or broad shared indexes when sensitive

00:01:10.570 --> 00:01:12.005
content is involved.

00:01:12.005 --> 00:01:14.560
Test permission boundaries before launch, after source changes,

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:17.240
and whenever the retrieval architecture changes.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:20.320
Access tests should use realistic roles and document examples.

00:01:20.320 --> 00:01:22.915
Include users with partial access, users from other tenants,

00:01:22.915 --> 00:01:24.840
users whose access was recently removed,

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:28.174
and service identities that support background jobs or evaluation workflows.
